-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 1: Modbus TCP/RTU Register Mapping for BAS Integration
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'how-to'),
  'Modbus TCP/RTU Register Mapping for BAS Integration',
  'modbus-tcp-rtu-register-mapping-bas-integration',
  'Practical guide to integrating Modbus TCP and RTU devices into building automation systems, covering register types, function codes, addressing conventions, and common field issues.',
  E'# Modbus TCP/RTU Register Mapping for BAS Integration\n\nModbus is one of the most common protocols for integrating third-party equipment into a BAS. Originally developed by Modicon (now Schneider Electric) in 1979, Modbus remains widely used for VFDs, power meters, boilers, chillers, and other equipment that does not natively speak BACnet.\n\n## Modbus Variants\n\n| Variant | Transport | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Modbus RTU | RS-485 serial | Field devices, older equipment |\n| Modbus ASCII | RS-485 serial | Rare; slower than RTU |\n| Modbus TCP | Ethernet (TCP port 502) | Modern equipment, higher bandwidth |\n\n## Register Types\n\nModbus defines four data areas, each with a different address range and read/write capability:\n\n| Data Type | Address Range | Access | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Coils | 0xxxx (0–65535) | Read/Write | Binary outputs (on/off commands) |\n| Discrete Inputs | 1xxxx (0–65535) | Read Only | Binary inputs (status, alarms) |\n| Input Registers | 3xxxx (0–65535) | Read Only | Analog inputs (measurements) |\n| Holding Registers | 4xxxx (0–65535) | Read/Write | Analog outputs, configuration |\n\nThe 0xxxx/1xxxx/3xxxx/4xxxx prefixes are a documentation convention. The actual Modbus PDU uses zero-based addressing without the prefix. Check whether your BAS platform uses zero-based or one-based addressing, as this is a frequent source of off-by-one errors.\n\n## Function Codes\n\nThe most common Modbus function codes used in BAS integrations:\n\n| Code | Name | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| 01 | Read Coils | Read binary output states |\n| 02 | Read Discrete Inputs | Read binary input states |\n| 03 | Read Holding Registers | Read analog values and configuration |\n| 04 | Read Input Registers | Read analog measurements |\n| 05 | Write Single Coil | Command a single binary output |\n| 06 | Write Single Register | Write a single analog value |\n| 15 | Write Multiple Coils | Command multiple binary outputs |\n| 16 | Write Multiple Registers | Write multiple analog values |\n\n## Data Type Handling\n\nModbus registers are 16-bit. Larger data types span multiple registers:\n\n| Data Type | Registers | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| 16-bit integer | 1 | Signed or unsigned |\n| 32-bit integer | 2 | Check byte order (big-endian vs little-endian) |\n| 32-bit float (IEEE 754) | 2 | Check word order (AB CD vs CD AB) |\n| 64-bit double | 4 | Rare in BAS applications |\n\nWord order and byte order are the most common source of garbled readings when integrating Modbus devices. If a value reads as nonsensical, try swapping the word order before assuming a wiring or hardware issue.\n\n## RS-485 Wiring for Modbus RTU\n\n| Parameter | Specification |\n|---|---|---|\n| Topology | Daisy chain (no star or T-taps) |\n| Cable | Shielded twisted pair |\n| Termination | 120 ohm at each end of the bus |\n| Max devices | 32 per segment (without repeaters) |\n| Max distance | 1200 m (4000 ft) at 9600 baud |\n| Common baud rates | 9600, 19200, 38400 |\n| Data format | 8 data bits, 1 stop bit, no parity (8N1) or even parity (8E1) |\n\nAll devices on an RS-485 bus must share the same baud rate, data bits, parity, and stop bit settings. A single device with mismatched serial settings can disrupt the entire bus.\n\n## Modbus TCP Considerations\n\n| Parameter | Detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| Default port | TCP 502 |\n| Unit ID | Identifies the target device (1–247) |\n| Connection limit | Many devices limit simultaneous TCP connections (often 4–8) |\n| Timeout | Set polling timeout appropriate to network latency |\n\nFor Modbus TCP, the Unit ID field is still relevant when communicating through gateways that bridge TCP to RTU. The gateway uses the Unit ID to route requests to the correct serial device.\n\n## Integration Steps\n\n### Step 1: Obtain the Modbus Register Map\n\nRequest the register map from the equipment manufacturer. This document lists every register address, data type, scaling factor, and access mode. Without it, integration is guesswork.\n\n### Step 2: Confirm Communication Parameters\n\nFor RTU: baud rate, parity, stop bits, device address.\nFor TCP: IP address, port (usually 502), Unit ID.\n\n### Step 3: Test with a Modbus Scanner\n\nBefore configuring the BAS, use a standalone Modbus polling tool to verify you can read registers. This isolates communication issues from BAS configuration issues.\n\n### Step 4: Configure the BAS Modbus Driver\n\nMap each register to a BAS point:\n\n- Set the correct function code\n- Set the register address (watch for zero-based vs one-based)\n- Set the data type and word order\n- Apply scaling if the register uses raw counts (e.g., value / 10 for one decimal place)\n- Set appropriate polling intervals\n\n### Step 5: Verify Readings Against the Device Display\n\nCompare BAS readings to the equipment''s local display or diagnostic screen. Check at least three operating conditions (low, mid, high) to confirm scaling is correct.\n\n## Common Field Issues\n\n| Issue | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| All values read as zero | Wrong slave address or no communication | Verify address, wiring, baud rate |\n| Values are garbled or extremely large | Wrong word order or byte order | Swap word order; verify data type |\n| Values are off by a factor of 10 or 100 | Missing or wrong scaling factor | Apply the scaling from the register map |\n| Intermittent communication drops | Wiring issue or baud rate mismatch | Check termination, polarity, baud rate |\n| Off-by-one register addresses | Zero-based vs one-based addressing | Check platform addressing convention |\n| Cannot write to registers | Wrong function code or register is read-only | Verify register access mode |\n| Communication works for some devices, not others | Duplicate slave addresses | Audit all device addresses on the bus |\n\n## References\n\n- Modbus Organization, "Modbus Application Protocol Specification V1.1b3" (modbus.org)\n- Modbus Organization, "Modbus over Serial Line Specification and Implementation Guide V1.02" (modbus.org)\n- ASHRAE Guideline 13-2015, "Specifying Direct Digital Control Systems"',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

