-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 2: BACnet Priority Array: How Commanding Works
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'BACnet Priority Array: How Commanding Works',
  'bacnet-priority-array-commanding',
  'Reference guide to the BACnet 16-level priority array, covering priority assignments, relinquish default behavior, common commanding mistakes, and practical examples.',
  E'# BACnet Priority Array: How Commanding Works\n\nThe priority array is fundamental to how BACnet commandable objects (Analog Output, Binary Output, Analog Value, Binary Value, and others designated commandable) resolve which value controls the physical output. ASHRAE Standard 135 defines a 16-level priority scheme where lower numbers take precedence over higher numbers.\n\n## The 16 Priority Levels\n\nDefined in ASHRAE Standard 135-2020, Clause 19:\n\n| Priority | Name | Typical Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Manual-Life Safety | Fire, smoke, critical safety overrides |\n| 2 | Automatic-Life Safety | Automatic safety sequences |\n| 3 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 4 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 5 | Critical Equipment Control | Equipment protection interlocks |\n| 6 | Minimum On/Off | Minimum runtime enforcement |\n| 7 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 8 | Manual Operator | Operator overrides from the front-end |\n| 9 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 10 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 11 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 12 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 13 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 14 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 15 | Available | Not commonly used |\n| 16 | Available | Default schedule/program writes |\n\nPriority 1 is the highest priority. Priority 16 is the lowest.\n\n## How the Priority Array Resolves\n\nThe controller evaluates the priority array from priority 1 down to priority 16. The first non-NULL entry wins and becomes the Present Value of the object.\n\n```\nPriority Array Example:\n  1: NULL\n  2: NULL\n  3: NULL\n  4: NULL\n  5: NULL\n  6: NULL\n  7: NULL\n  8: 72.0    <-- Operator override at priority 8\n  9: NULL\n  10: NULL\n  11: NULL\n  12: NULL\n  13: NULL\n  14: NULL\n  15: NULL\n  16: 68.0    <-- Schedule write at priority 16\n\nResult: Present Value = 72.0 (priority 8 wins)\n```\n\n## Relinquish Default\n\nIf all 16 priority slots are NULL, the object uses its Relinquish Default value. This is the fallback value that ensures the output always has a defined state.\n\n```\nAll priorities NULL:\n  Priority 1-16: NULL\n  Relinquish Default: 65.0\n\nResult: Present Value = 65.0\n```\n\nThe Relinquish Default is configured as a property of the commandable object, not as a priority level.\n\n## Writing and Relinquishing\n\n### Writing a Value\n\nTo command an object, a BACnet client writes a value to the Present Value property at a specific priority:\n\n```\nWriteProperty:\n  Object: Analog Output 1\n  Property: Present Value\n  Value: 75.0\n  Priority: 8\n```\n\nThis places 75.0 at priority 8 in the array.\n\n### Relinquishing a Value\n\nTo release a command, write NULL to the Present Value at the same priority:\n\n```\nWriteProperty:\n  Object: Analog Output 1\n  Property: Present Value\n  Value: NULL\n  Priority: 8\n```\n\nThis clears priority 8, and the next non-NULL priority (or the Relinquish Default) takes effect.\n\n## Common Commanding Mistakes\n\n| Mistake | What Happens | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Writing at priority 8, expecting schedule to work | Priority 8 overrides priority 16 schedule | Relinquish priority 8 when override is no longer needed |\n| Forgetting to relinquish after troubleshooting | Point stays stuck at override value indefinitely | Always relinquish after testing |\n| Writing at priority 1 from software | Blocks all other control, including safety | Reserve priorities 1-2 for life safety hardware |\n| Different systems writing at the same priority | Last writer wins; unpredictable behavior | Assign dedicated priorities per system |\n| Not checking the priority array when troubleshooting | Cannot determine why a point is at an unexpected value | Read the full priority array first |\n\n## Priority Assignment Best Practices\n\nA common convention for BAS projects:\n\n| Priority | Assignment |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Life safety hardwired interlocks only |\n| 2 | Automatic life safety sequences |\n| 5 | Equipment protection (freeze protection, high limit) |\n| 6 | Minimum on/off timers |\n| 8 | Operator overrides via the front-end |\n| 9 | Emergency demand response / load shed |\n| 12 | Optimal start/stop programs |\n| 14 | BAS control programs (PID loops, sequences) |\n| 16 | Schedules |\n\nDocument the priority assignment scheme in the project Basis of Design and enforce it consistently across all controllers and sequences.\n\n## Reading the Priority Array\n\nTo diagnose commanding issues, read the Priority Array property (not just Present Value):\n\n```\nReadProperty:\n  Object: Binary Output 1\n  Property: Priority Array\n\nResponse:\n  1: NULL\n  2: NULL\n  ...\n  8: ACTIVE    <-- Operator override holding the fan ON\n  ...\n  16: INACTIVE  <-- Schedule says fan should be OFF\n\nPresent Value: ACTIVE (priority 8 wins)\n```\n\nThis immediately shows that an operator override at priority 8 is preventing the schedule from controlling the fan.\n\n## Interaction with COV and Alarms\n\nWhen a higher priority overrides a lower one, the Present Value changes, which triggers:\n\n- COV notifications to subscribed clients\n- Alarm evaluation (if alarm extensions are configured)\n- History recording (if trended)\n\nThis is expected behavior. If an operator override causes alarm flooding, the solution is alarm rationalization, not suppressing the priority mechanism.\n\n## References\n\n- ASHRAE Standard 135-2020, "BACnet: A Data Communication Protocol for Building Automation and Control Networks," Clause 19 (Priority Array and Relinquish Default)\n- BACnet International, "BACnet Overview" (bacnet.org)\n- ASHRAE Guideline 13-2015, "Specifying Direct Digital Control Systems"',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

