-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 3: ASHRAE Guideline 36 High-Performance Sequences Overview
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'reference'),
  'ASHRAE Guideline 36: High-Performance Sequences of Operation',
  'ashrae-guideline-36-high-performance-sequences',
  'Overview of ASHRAE Guideline 36, covering standardized control sequences for VAV systems, dedicated outdoor air systems, and key concepts like trim-and-respond logic.',
  E'# ASHRAE Guideline 36: High-Performance Sequences of Operation\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36, "High-Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems," provides standardized, pre-engineered control sequences designed to deliver consistent energy performance across buildings. First published in 2018 and updated in 2021, it replaces ad-hoc, contractor-specific sequences with a common language for specifiers, programmers, and commissioning agents.\n\n## Why Guideline 36 Matters\n\nTraditional BAS sequences vary widely between contractors, leading to:\n\n- Inconsistent energy performance across similar buildings\n- Difficulty comparing bids when sequences differ\n- Commissioning agents verifying unique logic on every project\n- No established baseline for fault detection and diagnostics\n\nGuideline 36 addresses these by defining sequences that are:\n\n- Published, peer-reviewed, and publicly available through ASHRAE\n- Structured for direct implementation in BAS controllers\n- Designed around measured energy performance, not convention\n- Compatible with automated fault detection tools\n\n## Systems Covered\n\nASHRAE Guideline 36-2021 provides sequences for:\n\n| System Type | Key Sequences |\n|---|---|---|\n| Single-Zone VAV AHU | Supply air temperature reset, fan speed control, economizer |\n| Multi-Zone VAV AHU | Static pressure reset, supply air temperature reset, economizer, minimum outdoor air |\n| VAV Terminal Units | Cooling-only, reheat, dual-duct, fan-powered |\n| Dedicated Outdoor Air Systems (DOAS) | Supply air temperature control, demand-based ventilation |\n| Chilled and Hot Water Plants | Staging, reset, differential pressure control |\n| Zone-Level Controls | Occupied/unoccupied modes, setpoint adjustment, CO2-based ventilation |\n\n## Key Concepts\n\n### Trim-and-Respond (T&R) Logic\n\nTrim-and-respond replaces traditional PID-based reset strategies for duct static pressure and supply air temperature. Instead of using a single sensor, T&R uses zone-level feedback:\n\n```\nTrim-and-Respond Logic (simplified):\n\n  Every sample period (e.g., 2 minutes):\n    IF all zones are satisfied:\n      Decrease setpoint by trim amount (e.g., -0.05 in. WC)\n    ELSE IF any zone has an unmet demand:\n      Increase setpoint by respond amount (e.g., +0.15 in. WC)\n\n  Setpoint is bounded by configured min and max limits.\n```\n\nThe respond amount is intentionally larger than the trim amount, so the setpoint reacts quickly to unmet demand but slowly reduces when all zones are satisfied. This drives energy savings by keeping setpoints as low as possible while maintaining comfort.\n\n### Guideline 36 vs ASHRAE Standard 90.1\n\nGuideline 36 sequences typically exceed the minimum requirements of Standard 90.1:\n\n| Requirement | Standard 90.1 | Guideline 36 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Static pressure reset | Required above a size threshold | Trim-and-respond with zone feedback |\n| Supply air temp reset | Required above a size threshold | Trim-and-respond with zone feedback |\n| Economizer | Required by climate zone and capacity | Detailed staging and enable/disable logic |\n| Demand-controlled ventilation | Required above thresholds | CO2-based with zone-level calculations |\n| Optimum start | Required | Adaptive algorithm based on historical data |\n\n### Zone Sequence of Operation\n\nGuideline 36 defines standardized zone control modes:\n\n| Mode | Conditions | Actions |\n|---|---|---|\n| Heating | Zone temp below heating setpoint | Open reheat valve, minimum airflow |\n| Deadband | Zone temp between heating and cooling setpoints | Minimum ventilation airflow |\n| Cooling | Zone temp above cooling setpoint | Modulate damper, close reheat valve |\n| Unoccupied | Outside occupied schedule | Setback setpoints, minimum ventilation off |\n| Warmup | Transitioning from unoccupied to occupied | Maximum heating, no ventilation requirement |\n| Cooldown | Transitioning from unoccupied to occupied | Maximum cooling, no ventilation requirement |\n\n## Implementation Considerations\n\n### Controller Requirements\n\nGuideline 36 sequences assume modern DDC controllers with:\n\n- Sufficient I/O and processing for the defined logic\n- Support for network variables (zone requests communicated to AHU)\n- Ability to implement trim-and-respond loops\n- Trending capability for commissioning verification\n\n### Specifying Guideline 36\n\nTo specify Guideline 36 on a project:\n\n1. Reference the specific Guideline 36 sections in the sequence of operation specification\n2. Identify which system types apply (single-zone VAV, multi-zone VAV, DOAS, etc.)\n3. Document any project-specific deviations from the guideline\n4. Include Guideline 36 verification procedures in the commissioning plan\n\n### Commissioning with Guideline 36\n\nBecause the sequences are standardized, commissioning becomes more repeatable:\n\n- Functional performance tests can reference specific guideline sections\n- Trim-and-respond parameters can be verified against published defaults\n- Fault detection rules map directly to expected sequence behavior\n- Trend data can be compared to expected operating patterns\n\n## Relationship to Other Standards\n\n| Standard | Relationship to Guideline 36 |\n|---|---|\n| ASHRAE Standard 62.1 | Guideline 36 ventilation logic implements 62.1 requirements |\n| ASHRAE Standard 90.1 | Guideline 36 meets or exceeds 90.1 control requirements |\n| ASHRAE Standard 55 | Guideline 36 setpoints align with 55 comfort criteria |\n| ASHRAE Standard 135 (BACnet) | Guideline 36 is implementation-neutral but maps well to BACnet objects |\n| ASHRAE Guideline 13 | Specifying DDC systems; complementary to Guideline 36 |\n\n## References\n\n- ASHRAE Guideline 36-2021, "High-Performance Sequences of Operation for HVAC Systems" (ashrae.org)\n- ASHRAE Standard 90.1-2022, "Energy Standard for Sites and Buildings Except Low-Rise Residential Buildings"\n- Taylor, S.T., "Resetting Setpoints Using Trim & Respond Logic," ASHRAE Journal, November 2015\n- ASHRAE Standard 62.1-2022, "Ventilation and Acceptable Indoor Air Quality"',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

