-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ARTICLE 4: BAS Alarm Rationalization Best Practices
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
INSERT INTO wiki_articles (category_id, title, slug, summary, content, is_published)
VALUES (
  (SELECT id FROM wiki_categories WHERE slug = 'best-practices'),
  'BAS Alarm Rationalization Best Practices',
  'bas-alarm-rationalization-best-practices',
  'Guide to reducing alarm fatigue in building automation systems using ISA-18.2 principles, covering alarm classification, performance metrics, and a practical rationalization workflow.',
  E'# BAS Alarm Rationalization Best Practices\n\nAlarm fatigue is one of the most persistent operational problems in building automation. When operators receive hundreds of alarms per day, they stop responding to any of them. Alarm rationalization is the systematic process of reviewing every alarm to determine whether it is necessary, correctly configured, and actionable.\n\n## The Problem: Alarm Overload\n\nA typical unmanaged BAS generates far more alarms than an operator can process:\n\n| Metric | Unmanaged System | ISA-18.2 Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alarms per operator per 10 min | 10–50+ | 1 maximum |\n| Standing (stale) alarms | 50–500+ | Fewer than 10 |\n| Nuisance alarms (% of total) | 30–80% | Less than 10% |\n| Alarms with documented response | 10–30% | 100% |\n\nThe ISA-18.2 standard (IEC 62682) defines these performance benchmarks for alarm systems across process and building industries.\n\n## Alarm Classification\n\nNot every abnormal condition should be an alarm. ISA-18.2 defines a hierarchy:\n\n| Category | Definition | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alarm | Requires operator action within a defined time | Equipment failure, safety limit |\n| Alert | Informational; no immediate action required | Filter nearing replacement, sensor drift |\n| Event | Logged for record; no notification needed | Equipment start/stop, schedule change |\n| Suppressed | Temporarily hidden during known conditions | Alarm during planned maintenance |\n\nMany BAS installations treat events and alerts as alarms, which is the primary driver of alarm overload.\n\n## Alarm Priority Levels\n\nAssign priorities based on the consequence of not responding:\n\n| Priority | Response Time | Consequence of Inaction | Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Critical | Immediate (<5 min) | Safety hazard, major equipment damage | Freeze condition, fire damper fault |\n| High | Within 30 minutes | Significant comfort or operational impact | Chiller failure, boiler lockout |\n| Medium | Within 2 hours | Moderate comfort or efficiency impact | Zone temperature deviation, VFD fault |\n| Low | Within 24 hours | Minor impact; can wait for next shift | Sensor out of range, minor schedule conflict |\n\n## The Rationalization Process\n\n### Step 1: Inventory All Alarms\n\nExport every configured alarm from the BAS, including:\n\n- Point name and description\n- Alarm type (high limit, low limit, status change, etc.)\n- Current setpoints and deadbands\n- Priority level\n- Notification routing\n- Historical alarm frequency\n\n### Step 2: Classify Each Alarm\n\nFor each alarm, answer:\n\n1. Is there a specific operator response required?\n2. What is the consequence if the operator does not respond?\n3. Is this alarm distinct from other alarms on the same equipment?\n4. Is the setpoint appropriate for actual operating conditions?\n5. Is the deadband sufficient to prevent chattering?\n\nIf the answer to question 1 is "no," the item should be reclassified as an alert or event.\n\n### Step 3: Eliminate Nuisance Alarms\n\nCommon sources of nuisance alarms:\n\n| Source | Fix |\n|---|---|\n| Setpoint too tight | Widen to reflect actual operating range |\n| Deadband too narrow | Increase to prevent chatter (typically 2-5% of span) |\n| Alarm on maintenance point | Reclassify as event or suppress during maintenance |\n| Duplicate alarms (same condition, multiple points) | Consolidate to a single alarm |\n| Alarm during normal startup/shutdown | Add time delay or mode-based suppression |\n| Stale alarms that never clear | Fix the underlying condition or adjust the threshold |\n\n### Step 4: Define Response Procedures\n\nEvery remaining alarm must have a documented response:\n\n```\nAlarm Response Template:\n  Alarm: AHU-1 Supply Air Temperature High\n  Priority: Medium\n  Setpoint: 65 deg F (cooling mode)\n  Possible Causes:\n    1. Cooling valve stuck closed\n    2. Chilled water supply temperature too high\n    3. Economizer not functioning\n  Operator Response:\n    1. Check cooling valve position command vs feedback\n    2. Verify chilled water supply temperature\n    3. Check economizer damper operation\n  Escalation: If unresolved in 2 hours, contact HVAC technician\n```\n\n### Step 5: Monitor and Maintain\n\nAlarm rationalization is not a one-time activity. Establish ongoing review:\n\n| Activity | Frequency |\n|---|---|\n| Review top 10 most frequent alarms | Monthly |\n| Audit standing (uncleared) alarms | Weekly |\n| Review alarm metrics against targets | Monthly |\n| Full alarm rationalization review | Annually |\n| Post-incident alarm review | After any significant event |\n\n## Alarm Metrics to Track\n\n| Metric | How to Calculate | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alarm rate | Total alarms / time period / operators on shift | Less than 6 per hour per operator |\n| Peak alarm rate | Maximum alarms in any 10-minute window | Less than 1 per operator |\n| Standing alarms | Count of alarms in active state for more than 24 hours | Fewer than 10 |\n| Nuisance alarm % | (Alarms with no operator action / total alarms) x 100 | Less than 10% |\n| Stale alarm % | (Alarms active more than 24 hours / total active) x 100 | Less than 5% |\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\n| Pitfall | Consequence |\n|---|---|\n| Alarming everything "just in case" | Operators ignore all alarms |\n| No documented response procedures | Operators do not know what to do |\n| Setting all alarms to "critical" | Priority loses meaning |\n| Never reviewing alarm performance | System degrades over time |\n| Suppressing alarms instead of fixing root causes | Problems remain hidden |\n\n## References\n\n- ISA-18.2-2016, "Management of Alarm Systems for the Process Industries" (isa.org)\n- IEC 62682:2014, "Management of Alarm Systems for the Process Industries"\n- ASHRAE Guideline 13-2015, "Specifying Direct Digital Control Systems"\n- Engineering Equipment and Materials Users Association (EEMUA) Publication 191, "Alarm Systems: A Guide to Design, Management and Procurement"',
  true
)
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

